const laoziSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('persona-text', 'An Old Master, an attributed voice, and a classic', [
    '“Laozi” can name an Old Master of later biography, the authority to whom a short text was attributed, the text itself, and eventually a divine figure in religious Daoist traditions. Those meanings should not be fused into one documentary life. The received Daodejing speaks without an autobiographical narrator and supplies no verifiable career. Sima Qian’s much later account makes Laozi a Zhou archivist who instructs Confucius and writes at a frontier pass, but even that historian records competing identifications. This article therefore treats Laozi as an attributed philosophical persona: culturally consequential, perhaps preserving memories of Lao Dan, yet not a securely recoverable single author.',
    'That caution changes rather than diminishes the philosophical question. Instead of asking what one sixth-century sage privately believed, readers can ask how collections associated with Laozi taught audiences to reconsider power, knowledge, desire, and order. The title Daodejing, commonly rendered Classic of the Way and Virtue, reflects later canonical status; early witnesses do not all present the same title, sequence, or division. “The Laozi” is thus a useful name for a transmitted textual tradition. Its compact sayings gained coherence through collection, editing, commentary, and repeated acts of interpretation, not through a biography that modern evidence can simply reconstruct.',
  ], ['confucius', 'zhuangzi'], ['Daodejing']),
  section('formation-manuscripts', 'A layered work before the received eighty-one chapters', [
    'The work probably developed through sayings and overlapping collections during the late Warring States period, although proposals about individual layers remain contested. The received arrangement has eighty-one chapters, conventionally divided into a Dao portion, chapters 1–37, and a De portion, chapters 38–81. Two silk witnesses buried at Mawangdui in 168 BCE place the De material before the Dao material and preserve many textual variants. They show a substantially recognizable Laozi, but not one whose sequence and wording had always been fixed. “Composite” here describes transmission and formation; it does not prove that every chapter lacks thematic relation to every other.',
    'The Guodian bamboo slips, excavated from a tomb sealed around 300 BCE, preserve roughly two thousand characters corresponding to parts of about thirty-one received chapters, in markedly different groupings and order. Scholars dispute whether these slips are selections from a larger work, an earlier nucleus, or several teaching collections. Their absence of much received political material cannot by itself reveal a lost author’s intention. Together with Mawangdui, however, they make a single-act authorship story difficult to sustain. A responsible reading names the witness when a variant matters and avoids treating the later chapter sequence as the only imaginable architecture of the text.',
  ], ['laozi'], ['Daodejing']),
  section('warring-states', 'Counsel amid Warring States competition', [
    'Much of the Laozi becomes intelligible against a world of interstate warfare, administrative expansion, court persuasion, and debate about how rulers could secure order. Confucian and Mohist thinkers defended rival programs of cultivated conduct, public standards, and benefit; other advisers refined techniques of command. The Laozi repeatedly asks whether deliberate schemes can intensify the disorder they promise to cure. Its language of sages and rulers therefore matters even when a passage also supports spiritual or cosmological reading. The text is neither simply a private meditation manual nor straightforward policy advice: it uses political failure to expose more general patterns of overreaching action.',
    'The modern label “Daoism” is helpful only with qualifications. Han historians later grouped texts and figures under daojia, a “family” or lineage concerned with dao; neither Laozi nor Zhuangzi announced membership in that retrospective school. Modern contrasts between “philosophical Daoism” and “religious Daoism” can orient beginners, but the traditions became deeply intertwined, and the Laozi acquired ritual, devotional, cosmological, and institutional lives. This page uses Daoist for those histories of classification and reception, not to claim that one founder established a uniform creed or that the early text already contained every teaching of later organized communities.',
  ], ['confucius', 'zhuangzi'], ['Daodejing']),
  section('dao-naming', 'Dao, naming, and the limits of a fixed account', [
    'Dao can mean a way, course, method, or guiding path. In the Laozi it also points toward the generative course through which the many things arise and change. Chapter 1 contrasts a dao that can be articulated with a constant dao, while chapters 25 and 34 describe an elusive source that precedes named things without behaving like a commanding personal ruler. These passages do not yield one uncontested metaphysical object. Some interpreters stress transcendence or mysticism; others understand dao as immanent process, patterned possibility, or a critical standpoint on humanly constructed ways. Translation should preserve that dispute rather than deciding it by capitalizing “Way.”',
    'The critique of naming is equally easy to exaggerate. A text made of carefully arranged language cannot coherently teach that all speech is worthless. Names distinguish and guide, but they can harden provisional contrasts into reality’s final structure. Chapter 2 shows paired evaluations generating one another; chapter 32 links naming with social order and knowing when to stop. The practical warning is that rulers, moralists, and scholars may mistake their classifications for mastery. Laozi’s terse images then work as counter-language: they redirect attention without pretending to exhaust their subject. Dao is not made irrational by exceeding a definition, and disciplined uncertainty is not the same as ignorance.',
  ], ['laozi'], ['Daodejing']),
  section('de-wu-you', 'De, absence, and presence', [
    'De is often translated “virtue,” but that English word can imply moral respectability more narrowly than the Laozi allows. De is also potency, efficacy, or the way dao becomes effective in a person or thing. Chapter 38 contrasts higher de, which does not cling to being virtuous, with increasingly deliberate moral performance. This is not proof that kindness or justice are bad. It asks whether advertised goodness has become compensatory—an effort to simulate order after responsive conduct has failed. The sage’s de is visible less as possession or prestige than as the capacity to nourish without dominating and to accomplish without claiming ownership.',
    'The paired terms wu and you are likewise translation-sensitive. Depending on context, they can suggest absence and presence, not-having and having, or nonbeing and being. Chapter 11 uses the empty parts of wheel, vessel, and room to show how absence can enable use; chapter 40 relates the coming-to-be of what is present to what is absent. These claims need not establish creation from absolute nothingness. They invite readers to notice relations that object-centered attention misses: intervals, receptivity, indeterminacy, and unoccupied capacity. Later commentaries built powerful metaphysics from such lines, but their systems should be identified as interpretations, not smuggled into every early occurrence.',
  ], ['laozi'], ['Daodejing']),
  section('wuwei-ziran', 'Wuwei and ziran: action without coercive forcing', [
    'Wuwei literally joins “not” with “acting,” yet “doing nothing” is usually a poor guide. In chapters 3, 37, and 57 the sage or ruler acts without the assertive interventions that make people competitive, dependent, or evasive. The ideal can include restraint, unobtrusive action, and allowing processes to unfold without a self-important design. It is not a promise that effort, planning, or institutions are always corrupt. The harder question is diagnostic: when does action answer a situation, and when does it impose an agenda that multiplies resistance? Wuwei names an achievement of responsiveness, not a universal alibi for passivity.',
    'Ziran is often rendered “naturalness” or “spontaneity,” but its components suggest being “so of itself.” Chapter 25 says dao follows ziran, making it difficult to imagine ziran as an external law that dao obeys. The term directs attention to unforced emergence rather than conformity to a romantic wilderness. Nor does spontaneity mean obeying every impulse; impulses can be trained by status, fear, and acquisitiveness. Chapters 19 and 28 connect a simpler condition with undoing cultivated display and returning toward the uncarved block. The proposal is ethically demanding: reduce the compulsions by which a socially manufactured self mistakes its own striving for freedom.',
  ], ['laozi', 'zhuangzi'], ['Daodejing']),
  section('images-reversal', 'Water, softness, and reversal as philosophical method', [
    'Water gives the text one of its richest models. In chapter 8 it benefits things while occupying low places that others disdain; chapter 78 makes its softness an answer to hardness. The image does not supply a mechanical rule that yielding always wins. Water adapts, persists, receives contours, and can wear down what seems stronger. It changes what counts as efficacy. Valleys, infants, roots, and the feminine perform related work by locating generative power in what hierarchical judgment calls low or weak. Readers should not reduce these gendered images to claims about actual women, yet neither should they ignore how the text reverses conventionally valued dominance.',
    'Reversal, or return, is more than a surprising slogan. Chapter 22 links bending with preservation and not displaying oneself with recognition; chapter 76 observes that living things are supple while the dead are rigid. Such pairings disclose feedback: expansion invites exhaustion, possession invites loss, and triumph creates the conditions of defeat. The argument is not that every opposite inevitably replaces its partner on a schedule. It is a discipline of attending to limits and unintended consequences. The sage does not seek weakness for its own sake but refuses to confuse brittleness with strength or relentless advance with durable achievement.',
  ], ['laozi', 'zhuangzi'], ['Daodejing']),
  section('politics-war', 'Low-profile rule, livelihood, and the danger of weapons', [
    'The Laozi’s politics combine concern for ordinary livelihood with profound suspicion of governing display. Chapters 57 and 75 associate proliferating prohibitions, cleverness, and extraction with disorder and hunger. Chapter 60 compares governing a large state to handling a small fish: too much manipulation ruins what is being governed. The ideal ruler reduces burdens and does not convert every social problem into an occasion for self-magnifying control. Yet these lines support competing readings. They can counsel a ruler in subtler statecraft, criticize centralized ambition, or imagine a community less penetrated by rule. Calling the text simply libertarian or authoritarian erases that unresolved political range.',
    'Weapons receive no heroic aura. Chapters 30 and 31 treat military force as ominous and victory as an occasion for mourning, while chapter 80 imagines a small community unwilling to use its weapons or travel toward neighboring states. These passages strongly criticize conquest, but they do not constitute a detailed modern theory of justified war or civil rights. Nor is the small-state image automatically a practical blueprint; it may expose the desires and mobilizations on which empire depends. The continuing challenge is whether noninterference protects vulnerable people or merely asks them to endure existing power—a problem the text provokes without fully resolving.',
  ], ['laozi'], ['Daodejing']),
  section('comparison-reception', 'Not one doctrine: Confucian comparison and later readings', [
    'The Laozi often criticizes deliberate benevolence, righteousness, ritual display, and cultivated cleverness, terms central to early debates involving Confucians and Mohists. It can sound as if named virtues are symptoms appearing after dao has been lost. Still, the traditions share worries about corrupt rulers, disordered desire, and self-cultivation. “Daoist spontaneity versus Confucian ritual” is therefore a useful opening contrast, not a final map. The received text may also contain multiple voices whose political and cosmological emphases differ. Comparison works best passage by passage, without inventing a face-to-face contest between a historically secure Confucius and an equally secure author named Laozi.',
    'Commentaries made the work new in changing settings. The Heshang Gong tradition links it with cultivation and governance; Wang Bi reads dao, nonbeing, and naturalness through a highly influential account of order; later religious communities revered Laozi and embedded the classic in revelations, liturgies, ethics, and practices. Chinese Buddhists and literati also read its language through their own questions. These afterlives are not contamination of a pure philosophical original. They are evidence of textual openness, though they must be dated. A claim made by a third-century commentator or a later Daoist scripture cannot automatically be assigned to the earliest recoverable collection.',
  ], ['confucius', 'zhuangzi'], ['Daodejing']),
  section('misreadings', 'Productive disagreements and common misreadings', [
    'Three shortcuts repeatedly distort the text. Wuwei is not simple inactivity; the nameless dao is not straightforwardly a supernatural creator; and simplicity is not permission to despise learning or technology in every form. More serious interpretations disagree about whether the work is primarily mystical, political, ethical, therapeutic, cosmological, or a layered combination. They also disagree about unity: recurring images can support a coherent vision without proving one author or one doctrine. The correct response is not to declare every reading equal, but to test interpretations against particular chapters, manuscript variants, early context, and the explanatory work each translated term is being asked to perform.',
    'Modern admiration creates additional risks. A slogan detached from its chapter can turn Laozi into a management consultant, environmental prophet, pacifist, or advocate of effortless personal success. Some comparisons illuminate genuine questions; none should replace the Warring States problems of rule, naming, warfare, and cultivation. “Nature” can also mislead if it suggests a realm wholly outside human activity. Ziran concerns unforced becoming, while dao includes paths along which lives and communities proceed. The text’s relevance lies not in anticipating a modern ideology, but in its sustained challenge to domination, manufactured desire, and certainty about the benefits of intervention.',
  ], ['laozi', 'zhuangzi'], ['Daodejing']),
  section('reading-path', 'A source-conscious route through the Daodejing', [
    'Begin with chapters 1, 8, 11, 22, 25, 37, 38, 57, 67, 76, 78, and 80 in two named modern translations. Record how each handles dao, de, wu, wuwei, and ziran rather than assembling a hybrid English text from memorable lines. Then compare the order and variants reported for Mawangdui and Guodian. The goal is not to crown the oldest witness automatically correct, but to see how sequence changes interpretation. Read Alan Chan’s specialist overview beside the text for the biography, manuscript, commentary, and interpretive questions that a translation alone cannot settle.',
    'A second stage should pair the Laozi with the Analects and selected Inner Chapters of the Zhuangzi. Ask which criticisms require a specifically Confucian target, which address statecraft more broadly, and which later became signs of a shared Daoist tradition. Follow with Wang Bi or Heshang Gong in a scholarly translation to experience reception rather than treating commentary as noise. The best beginner conclusion is provisional: Laozi names an attributed voice transmitted through layered texts and communities, while the Daodejing teaches readers to examine how aggressive control, fixed naming, and the pursuit of visible strength can defeat their own purposes.',
  ], ['confucius', 'zhuangzi'], ['Daodejing', 'Analects', 'Zhuangzi']),
];

