const zhuangziSections: ArticleSection[] = [
  section('person-collection', 'Master Zhuang and a book larger than one author', [
    '“Zhuangzi” names both Master Zhuang, conventionally identified as Zhuang Zhou, and the book transmitted under his title. The person probably flourished in the later fourth century BCE, but most biographical detail comes from stories in a collection that freely mixes history, fantasy, parody, and invented dialogue. Later reports place him in Meng and connect him with a minor post and with Hui Shi; none yields a continuous documentary life. This article uses Zhuangzi for the persona when context is clear and “the Zhuangzi” for the composite text.',
    'That distinction matters because the received book contains voices from more than one period. It would be misleading to write “Zhuangzi believed” whenever any of its thirty-three chapters speaks. The safest phrasing attributes claims to a named chapter, to the Inner Chapters as a group, or to the received collection. Even the Inner Chapters cannot be signed like a modern author’s manuscript: they are traditionally closest to Zhuang Zhou, but scholars dispute their dating, unity, and relation to later layers.',
  ], ['hui-shi', 'laozi'], ['Zhuangzi']),
  section('text-history', 'Inner, Outer, and Miscellaneous chapters', [
    'Guo Xiang, who died in 312 CE, shaped the recension on which the received Zhuangzi depends. He reports reducing a fifty-two-chapter collection to thirty-three chapters and arranged them as seven Inner, fifteen Outer, and eleven Miscellaneous chapters. His commentary became a major interpretive presence and transmission vehicle. The three labels organize the received book; they do not by themselves prove exact authorship, date, or school. Guo’s confidence that the Inner Chapters belonged to Zhuang Zhou is evidence in reception, not contemporary certification.',
    'Textual scholarship identifies recurring vocabularies and positions across the later chapters, but classifications remain arguments. Liu Xiaogan’s linguistic and comparative study supports priority for the Inner Chapters and distinguishes groups within Outer and Miscellaneous materials; A. C. Graham and Harold Roth offer partly different strata. Some chapters combine fragments rather than presenting one sustained voice. A composite anthology may preserve debate, adaptation, and criticism within a developing Zhuangzi lineage.',
  ], ['zhuangzi'], ['Zhuangzi']),
  section('warring-states-label', 'A Warring States intervention before “Daoism”', [
    'The strongest early materials belong to the competitive intellectual world of the Warring States. Confucians and Mohists argued over ritual, care, benefit, standards, and government; technical disputers analyzed names and distinctions; rulers sought administrative and military advantage. The Zhuangzi knows that field rather than speaking from an untouched wilderness. Hui Shi appears repeatedly as friend and foil, and the text turns contemporary problems about language, knowledge, and guidance into comic drama. Its refusal of a ruler’s employment is intelligible partly as resistance to the demand that intellectual skill serve dangerous courts, not merely as a timeless celebration of private individuality.',
    'Calling the work Daoist is historically useful but retrospective. Han bibliographers grouped Laozi, Zhuangzi, and other materials as daojia, while later philosophical and religious traditions made the two masters a canonical pair. The Zhuangzi itself does not declare allegiance to a school founded by Laozi, and its use of dao need not duplicate the Daodejing. It often treats many ways—human and nonhuman, inherited and improvised—rather than only one cosmic Way. “Classical Daoist” here marks family resemblance and reception, with real differences preserved. The modern philosophical/religious split likewise cannot cleanly partition a book that later shaped commentary, cultivation, ritual traditions, literature, and Buddhist dialogue.',
  ], ['laozi', 'confucius', 'mozi'], ['Zhuangzi', 'Daodejing']),
  section('literary-method', 'Parable, humor, and voices that refuse a final perch', [
    'The Zhuangzi philosophizes through form. Giant birds, tiny insects, damaged bodies, talking trees, skulls, dreams, artisans, and impossible conversations make a reader inhabit a judgment before seeing its limits. These are not decorative illustrations for doctrines stated elsewhere. Chapter 1 changes scale until ordinary measures of large, small, useful, and successful wobble; chapter 2 lets speakers reverse positions while asking who could serve as an impartial judge. Humor lowers the prestige of solemn certainty, and the narrator never becomes an unquestionable authority.',
    'Its language is deliberately polysemous. Names, puns, sounds, and allusions can support several English renderings, while pronouns and syntax may leave a speaker’s target open. A smooth translation inevitably chooses. Comparing translations is consequently philosophical work, not pedantry. Terms such as dao, de, tian, ziran, and wuwei overlap with Laozi vocabulary but function in different argumentative and narrative settings. “Wandering,” “equalizing,” “nourishing life,” and “fasting the heart-mind” are also interpretive renderings, not transparent labels. A good summary names the chapter and preserves alternative readings where a lexical choice would predetermine the doctrine.',
  ], ['zhuangzi'], ['Zhuangzi']),
  section('perspectives-language', 'Perspectives, distinctions, and the pivot of dao', [
    'Chapter 2 probes judgments often represented by shi and fei—this/right/affirming and not-this/wrong/denying. Creatures and communities divide the world from situated forms of life, while disputers forget that their own language is one practice among others. The text does not merely announce that words fail. It deploys extraordinary verbal skill to reveal how naming guides attention and action. Its “pivot of dao” suggests responsiveness not exhausted by taking one side as final. One can use distinctions without becoming possessed by them, recognizing that an opposing claim may disclose features made invisible by one’s present path.',
    'This perspectivism is not automatically the modern thesis that all beliefs are equally true. The Zhuangzi contrasts cramped standpoints with wider roaming, ridicules dangerous confidence, and values capacities for adaptive response. Yet interpreters dispute whether it offers positive standards, therapeutic skepticism, relativism, pluralism, mystical illumination, or ironic suspension among these. The chapter’s imagined debate over who could adjudicate rival claims exposes the difficulty of a neutral standpoint, not the uselessness of inquiry. Epistemic modesty follows: finite lives and learned vocabularies do not survey every possible dao. That modesty can encourage listening and experimentation without guaranteeing a view from nowhere.',
  ], ['hui-shi'], ['Zhuangzi']),
  section('knowledge-skill', 'Knowing how: Cook Ding and trained responsiveness', [
    'Chapter 3’s Cook Ding describes cutting an ox with a blade that finds spaces rather than battering through joints. The story is often used to celebrate effortless expertise, but its details resist a fantasy of automatic flow. Ding began with undifferentiated visual attention, underwent years of practice, and still slows down when a difficult place appears. His activity joins perception, bodily skill, caution, and responsiveness to structure. The ruler who listens hears guidance for nourishing life, not a general command to become a butcher. Skilled action exemplifies a knowing-how that cannot be replaced by explicit rules, yet it is trained rather than miraculously innate.',
    'Other skill stories complicate the model. Artisans, swimmers, ferrymen, and cicada catchers display absorptive capacities, but talent can also attract dangerous usefulness. Wuwei and ziran in a Zhuangzian setting should not be translated as untrained impulse. Action can become “self-so” when practice no longer requires anxious self-monitoring while remaining alert to changing conditions. Expertise is local: Cook Ding knows this craft but does not acquire infallible access to every value.',
  ], ['zhuangzi'], ['Zhuangzi']),
  section('self-transformation', 'Fasting the heart-mind and loosening a fixed self', [
    'Chapter 4’s “fasting of the heart-mind” is offered in a politically dangerous scenario where ordinary persuasion may get the would-be adviser killed. The practice empties a crowded, calculating center so that response can attend through qi rather than forcing a preconceived plan. Whether this is meditation instruction, literary counsel, or both remains debated. It should not be reduced to blankness or obedience. The surrounding story keeps ethical and political risk visible: flexible attention may help a person survive coercive power, but no spiritual technique guarantees that a ruler will become just or that intervention will succeed.',
    'Chapter 6 pushes transformation further through figures who do not cling to one bodily form or socially approved identity. The “genuine person” is not an invulnerable superhero; the stories test whether the self can move with change without making its present boundaries absolute. This includes unsettling treatments of illness, disability, life, and death. Modern readers should resist turning bodily difference into a metaphor whose only purpose is able-bodied enlightenment. Several episodes instead reverse social judgment by giving de, friendship, or authority to people marked as deformed, while exposing institutions that define worth through appearance and usefulness.',
  ], ['confucius'], ['Zhuangzi']),
  section('death-dream', 'Butterfly, mourning, and the transformation of things', [
    'The butterfly dream at the end of chapter 2 does not simply prove that waking life is unreal. Zhuang Zhou dreams he is a butterfly, then wakes uncertain how the identities relate; the passage concludes with the transformation of things. Dream and waking remain distinguishable within experience even as neither supplies an unquestionable standpoint outside all transformation. Skeptical, mystical, and identity-centered readings each capture part of its force. What the episode reliably disrupts is complacent ownership of one fixed self. Its brevity makes it a test case for translation: small choices about knowing, dividing, and transformation can steer readers toward different philosophies.',
    'Death scenes extend that experiment without prescribing emotional numbness. In chapter 18, a story portrays Zhuangzi first grieving his wife and later drumming as he reflects on seasonal transformation; elsewhere friends respond to impending death with wonder or composure. These literary provocations question the demand that one phase of life be permanent. They do not establish that grief is irrational, that bereavement has one proper timetable, or that historical Zhuang Zhou behaved exactly as narrated. Acceptance and attachment remain in tension. The stories ask whether mourning can acknowledge loss without interpreting change as a cosmic violation directed against the self.',
  ], ['zhuangzi'], ['Zhuangzi']),
  section('use-disability', 'Uselessness, survival, and resistance to social measurement', [
    'Stories of useless trees and oversized gourds challenge measures built around immediate service. A gnarled tree survives because carpenters reject its timber; Hui Shi’s huge gourd becomes valuable once its imagined use changes. “The usefulness of uselessness” is not praise of incompetence. It asks whose project supplies the criterion. In a world of conscription, sacrifice, court employment, and extraction, being useful to power may destroy a life. Uselessness opens shelter and roaming outside dominant evaluation, while creative reframing discovers possibilities that a fixed tool-concept hid. The theme is political because states and professions decide which bodies and capacities count.',
    'Chapters 4 and 5 give socially stigmatized bodies speaking roles that expose the prejudice of normal form. Some survive levies because officials consider them unfit; others attract students through de rather than appearance. These passages can unsettle ableist judgment, but they also belong to an ancient text that sometimes instrumentalizes bodily difference for philosophical surprise. Responsible reception holds both facts. The goal is not to romanticize impairment as secret wisdom. It is to notice how beauty, productivity, ritual fitness, and wholeness are socially policed—and how the book imagines flourishing that does not ask permission from those standards.',
  ], ['confucius', 'hui-shi'], ['Zhuangzi']),
  section('politics-ethics', 'Withdrawal, improvisation, and political responsibility', [
    'The Zhuangzi often makes office look like a trap. In chapter 17, the turtle story prefers life in the mud to honored remains in a ruler’s shrine; other episodes show advisers risking death when they approach violent authorities. Refusal protects independence from court ambition and resists turning philosophy into a technique of domination. Yet the book is not politically empty. It diagnoses how status, profit, compulsory usefulness, punishment, and fixed norms deform lives. Some scholars also find ironic forms of commitment within detachment. The text offers no modern platform, but it repeatedly asks what intellectual integrity costs under coercive institutions.',
    'An ethical tension remains. Flexibility can make room for plural ways of life, but withdrawal may leave victims of power without assistance. Skepticism about universal standards can restrain zealotry, but it can also seem unable to condemn cruelty. Chapter 4 dramatizes rather than resolves the problem: engagement risks capture or death, while silence carries its own burden. Zhuangzian ethics may lie less in a rule than in capacities—attending, refusing possessive certainty, preserving life, improvising, and responding without fixing another being into one use. Those capacities need critical supplementation when structural injustice demands coordinated judgment and action.',
  ], ['zhuangzi'], ['Zhuangzi']),
  section('reception-modern', 'Laozi, Chan, and modern appropriations', [
    'Laozi and Zhuangzi became paired pillars of later Daoist philosophy, but reading the latter as a disciple’s elaboration of the former erases textual and conceptual differences. Zhuangzi’s multiple daos, comic disputation, and transformations do not simply illustrate the Daodejing’s aphorisms. Guo Xiang’s commentary, later religious Daoist readings, and encounters with Buddhist vocabulary reshaped the collection. Chinese Buddhist and especially Chan traditions found resources in its critique of fixation and its everyday skill imagery, while transforming them inside Buddhist accounts of practice and liberation. Influence is therefore reception and reconstruction, not evidence that the early Zhuangzi already taught Chan Buddhism.',
    'Modern readers recruit Zhuangzi for relativism, skepticism, psychotherapy, ecology, anarchism, disability critique, and aesthetics. These conversations can be philosophically fruitful when identified as extensions. They become misleading when the composite book is made to endorse a ready-made contemporary identity or when “nature” is imagined as a pristine realm outside culture. Ziran does not transparently mean modern authenticity, and wandering does not merely mean self-expression. The collection’s strangeness is an asset: it interrupts modern certainties too. Responsible comparison begins with a particular chapter, translation, and textual layer before asking what new work an ancient image can perform now.',
  ], ['laozi', 'nagarjuna'], ['Zhuangzi', 'Daodejing']),
  section('reading-path', 'Reading the voices without forcing them into one system', [
    'Begin with the seven Inner Chapters in a complete named translation, giving special attention to chapters 1–4 and 6. Read each story twice before extracting a thesis: first follow its emotional and comic movement, then ask which standard, speaker, or standpoint has shifted. Compare a second translation for chapter 2 and the butterfly dream, marking how it renders dao, shi-fei, tian, and transformation. Use the current SEP and IEP articles as contrasting interpretive maps rather than neutral substitutes for the text. Their differences demonstrate why “Zhuangzi was a relativist” is a question to investigate, not an introductory fact.',
    'Then sample Outer and Miscellaneous chapters—17, 18, 20, 26, and 33—while keeping their attribution distinct. Liu Xiaogan’s study shows how arguments about vocabulary, parallels, and chronology produce proposed groupings; it should sharpen uncertainty rather than replace one founder legend with a rigid modern taxonomy. Finally read Laozi beside the Zhuangzi and one early Confucian or Mohist text. Ask what each calls dao, which practices it distrusts, and whether freedom requires withdrawal, trained skill, wider perspective, or political reform. The best reading remains mobile without treating every interpretation as equally supported.',
  ], ['laozi', 'confucius', 'mozi', 'hui-shi'], ['Zhuangzi', 'Daodejing']),
];

